{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.85,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R1"],
      "pins": ["R1.1", "R1.2", "Q1.1", "Q2.3"],
      "nets": ["VCC", "Net-(Q1-G)"],
      "observed": "R1 is a 10 Ω resistor connected between the PMOS source rail VCC and the PMOS gate net Net-(Q1-G). Q2, a BC817 NPN transistor, pulls this same gate net toward ground when enabled.",
      "expected": "A gate-source pull-up for a high-side PMOS switch should be a high resistance, typically tens to hundreds of kΩ, so that the NPN driver only sinks a small gate current. The nearby pull-up resistors R3 and R6 are 10 K, supporting the expected order of magnitude.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"R1\") (value \"10\") (footprint \"Resistor_SMD:R_0805_2012Metric\")"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"7\") (name \"Net-(Q1-G)\") ... (node (ref \"Q1\") (pin \"1\") ... (node (ref \"Q2\") (pin \"3\") ... (node (ref \"R1\") (pin \"2\")"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"15\") (name \"VCC\") ... (node (ref \"Q1\") (pin \"2\") ... (node (ref \"R1\") (pin \"1\")"
        },
        {
          "source": "spice",
          "quote": "R1 VCC Net-_Q1-G_ 10"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"Q2\") (value \"BC817\") ... (libsource (lib \"Device\") (part \"Q_NPN_BEC\")"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "When Q2 turns on to activate the PMOS fan switch, the 10 Ω resistor forces a large DC current from VCC into the gate net and through Q2. Even at 5 V this is about 0.5 A, and at 12 V about 1.2 A, far beyond a BC817 SOT-23 transistor and an 0805 resistor. The board may overheat, fail, or not switch the fan correctly.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design contains a likely wrong-value anomaly: R1 is 10 Ω in the PMOS gate-source pull-up position, which would cause excessive current through Q2 and R1 when the fan switch is enabled."
}

